WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.984
This iPhone is mapping a room from a LiDAR on an Ethernet cable.

00:00:04.364 --> 00:00:04.965
No laptop.

00:00:04.965 --> 00:00:06.769
The phone runs the whole thing.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.125
Hi, I'm Plumbob.

00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:14.093
Mobile SLAM's core is eleven hundred and eighty lines of C plus plus.

00:00:14.093 --> 00:00:15.618
Here is what they do.

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:21.544
The sensor stamps points and IMU with its own clock.

00:00:21.544 --> 00:00:23.750
The phone's sensors never enter the estimate.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.069
A USB-C Ethernet adapter takes the address the sensor sends to.

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:31.002
Plain UDP sockets catch the packets.

00:00:31.002 --> 00:00:32.268
No Livox SDK.

00:00:32.668 --> 00:00:36.230
If the filter falls behind, point packets are dropped, never IMU.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.517
One worker thread feeds the filter.

00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:43.910
The IMU predicts the motion during the sweep, and every point is

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.845
moved to where it was really measured.

00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:48.936
Each point fits a plane through five map neighbours.

00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:51.310
Its residual is the distance to that plane.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.817
A gate drops points too far off, looser for distant ones.

00:00:55.237 --> 00:00:56.651
One cheap Kalman step.

00:00:56.651 --> 00:00:59.477
Re-associate and step again, at most four times.

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:01.821
Only then does the uncertainty shrink.

00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:07.535
Mapping starts with a second of standing still, to find the gyro

00:01:07.535 --> 00:01:09.140
bias and which way is down.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.326
Then you walk.

00:01:10.326 --> 00:01:13.281
Every tenth of a second, a sweep is deskewed, matched and

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:14.356
added to the map.

00:01:15.525 --> 00:01:20.083
FAST-LIO2 keeps a tree of nearby points and deletes what falls behind you.

00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:23.965
Mobile SLAM keeps one point per voxel in a hash table.

00:01:23.965 --> 00:01:25.854
Fast, tiny, and it only grows.

00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:31.312
To localise, the saved map is cut into one metre cells, and

00:01:31.312 --> 00:01:32.544
each becomes a Gaussian.

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:34.410
A scan arrives slightly off.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.591
Climbing the score pulls it into the cells, and the pose

00:01:37.591 --> 00:01:38.748
goes into the filter.

00:01:39.168 --> 00:01:42.676
With no heading, it tries twelve headings at five starting points.

00:01:44.768 --> 00:01:47.815
It refines the best and accepts it when enough points match.

00:01:50.408 --> 00:01:52.739
On a real Mid-360: mapping.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:56.184
And localising, with ninety-nine percent of points matched.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.741
For scan to BIM, the gaps are specific.

00:02:00.461 --> 00:02:03.413
No loop closure, so drift leaves walls drawn twice.

00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:06.078
One raw return per cell, noise and all.

00:02:06.986 --> 00:02:09.091
Fixed calibration, no control points.

00:02:09.918 --> 00:02:12.576
And a blind radius that hides most of a stair core.

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:17.471
Use the phone to record raw packets and check coverage on site.

00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:18.366
Map somewhere else.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.839
FAST-LIO's filter, rewritten small.

00:02:22.839 --> 00:02:24.294
NDT, fused properly.

00:02:24.294 --> 00:02:27.203
A logger, not a survey instrument.

00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:29.734
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:30.337
I'm Plumbob.

00:02:30.337 --> 00:02:30.639
Bye!
